From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 11:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] press the Start button on a clock of its own

A session can only be started by tapping the Start button, and the pointer
that has to carry that tap is the thing the stream window fights over: the
window owns the pointer while a stream runs, and the seat's own pointer only
appears with the first mouse event (patch 0009), so arming a run and leaving
it alone starts nothing. This patch puts a clock on the same button so a
session can be started without a pointer at all, which is what makes the
audio and the present rate measurable by themselves: the clock waits 60 s of
home screen and then calls root.startHero(), the very function the button's
TapHandler calls, so the launch path, its guards and its guards' order are
upstream's unchanged.

The wait is the app's start-up cost: the catalog must be loaded (ShellStore
ready) and the seat signed in before a launch does anything, and the same
60 s was the shortest settle measured here. --auto-launch-ms=<ms> sets the
wait and --auto-launch-ms=0 means nothing is armed, which is the default:
autoLaunchMs is 0, the Timer's running binding is false and the run is
upstream's, so no behaviour of r0.44 changes until the flag is passed.

AppController::reportAutoLaunch is the clock's only trace. QML logs nothing
in this application, and a clock that has not fired has no other symptom, so
an armed run that never launched would otherwise look identical to a run that
was never armed; the line says which, and the launch itself shows up as a
fresh session in native-streamer.log.

Greppable markers: opennow-autolaunch
